course: dictionary
lesson: 9940
title: Target: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it. Those words are the context, and they give you clues.
explain: A target can be a thing you aim at, like the circle in archery. It can be a goal you try to reach, like «a target of 20 books». It can also be a person or thing that is attacked or criticised. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence.
explain: As a verb, to target means to aim at or choose someone or something for an action. Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of the word. If it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
fact: A target is a thing you aim at and try to hit, like the circle in archery or darts.
fact: A target can also be a goal or result you try to reach, such as a reading target.
fact: A target can mean a person or thing that is attacked or criticised.
fact: As a verb, «target» means to aim at or choose someone or something for an action.
fact: «If the same person is always the target, it is picking on them, even if they laugh along.»
exercise: «The children took turns throwing darts at the target.» What is the target here? | A goal of 20 books | A person being picked on | *The circle you aim at | A rule about the race
exercise: Ms Patel says, «My target this week is to use capital letters.» What does target mean here? | To aim at someone with a ball | A thing you throw at | A person who gets blamed | *A goal you try to reach
exercise: «Scammers target older people online.» What does target mean in this sentence? | To hit a bullseye on a board | To laugh along with someone | To save money for later | *To choose someone to act against on purpose
test: «A saving goal (or target) is the thing or amount you are saving for.» In this sentence, what does target mean? | A person who is picking on others | Something you throw | *A thing or amount you are saving for | To aim a ball at a net
test: «If the same person is always the target, it is picking on them, even if they laugh along.» What does picking on them mean? | Giving them a prize | Playing a fair game | *Choosing them to be unfairly treated again and again | Being kind to them
exam: «Changing the target each time someone meets it is moving the goalposts; fair rules are clear before the race starts.» What does this sentence suggest is unfair? | Clear rules before the race starts | Starting a race on time | Writing the rules down | *Changing the target after someone has already met it
